---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {digitalChart,digitalNumbers} from '../../dossiers/digital-access.charts.js';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';import {digitalModel} from '../../dossiers/digital-access.model.js';import {digitalFormat} from '../../dossiers/digital-access.format.js';
const D=bySlug['digital-access'],model=digitalModel();
const N=digitalNumbers(model);
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const period=model.plates[0].period;
---
<Base title="Digital access and skills · Measure of India" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Digital access</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="/guides/digital-access/" ogImage="/og/digital-access.png">
<div class="page">
<div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="digital-access-cmst2025-18, digital-access-cmst2025-5, digital-access-cmst2025-3, digital-access-cmst2025-13" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Most Indian homes now have internet, almost always through a phone on mobile data. Who in the home goes online, who owns the phone and what they can do with it is a different story, and that is where women and villages fall behind.</p></div><div class="meta"><span>Survey period <b>{period}</b></span><span>Survey <b>Comprehensive Modular Survey: Telecom, NSS 80th round</b></span><span>People <b>Aged 15 and above unless a chart says otherwise</b></span></div>
<div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Homes with internet</div><div class="v acc" id="n-householdInternet">{N["n-householdInternet"]}</div><div class="d">Usable at home, on any network</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">People who went online</div><div class="v" id="n-personInternet">{N["n-personInternet"]}</div><div class="d">In the three months before the interview</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">People who own a mobile phone</div><div class="v" id="n-mobileOwnership">{N["n-mobileOwnership"]}</div><div class="d">On the day of the interview</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">People who can report a cybercrime online</div><div class="v" id="n-cyberReporting">{N["n-cyberReporting"]}</div><div class="d">By their own account</div></div></div></div>
<section data-inputs="digital-access-cmst2025-1, digital-access-cmst2025-3, digital-access-cmst2025-5, digital-access-cmst2025-18" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>One phone, a whole family</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>In many homes the internet arrives on one smartphone. The husband takes it to work, a child borrows it for homework in the evening, and the mother uses it when it is free. The home is counted as connected, but each person's access depends on who holds the phone.</p><p>That is why this survey asks separate questions of households and of people: whether the home has internet, whether each person used a phone or went online, whether they own a phone of their own, and what they can do once they are online.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Being online is also not the same as being able to use it. Sending a photo on a messaging app is common. Writing a letter on a computer, filling a form, reporting a fraud or paying a bill takes more, and many who go online every day cannot or do not do these things.</p><p>These skills matter more each year, as rations, scholarships, bank accounts and train tickets move to apps and websites.</p></div></div></section>
<section data-inputs="all digital-access bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read the survey</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Check who is counted.</b> Some figures are shares of homes, some of people. Some describe a narrower group: the kind of phone people own is a share of phone owners, how often people go online is a share of internet users. Each chart names its group.</p><p><b>Skills are what people say.</b> Nobody was tested. Some questions ask what a person did in the three months before the interview, others what they can do. A task not done may not have been needed.</p><p><b>Using is not owning.</b> A person who used a phone may have borrowed it. Ownership is asked separately, on the day of the interview.</p><p><b>All persons includes everyone.</b> The figures for all persons include people the survey does not show separately as men or women.</p><p><b>States carry more uncertainty.</b> The survey was designed for national figures. Each state chart has a table with the number of homes surveyed in the state and the sampling error of its estimate.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Not a count of connections</span>A share of people online is different from a telecom company's count of subscriptions. Many people have two SIMs; many share one phone.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A dash is a gap</span>Where the survey had no sample for a cut, the table shows a dash. A dash is never a zero.</aside></div></div></section>
<section id="current-reading" data-inputs="facts-digital-access" data-prose="data-bound snapshot facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the survey found</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{period}</span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Internet at home is now the norm: <b id="n-ruralHomeInternet">{N["n-ruralHomeInternet"]}</b> of village homes and <b id="n-urbanHomeInternet">{N["n-urbanHomeInternet"]}</b> of homes in towns. Among people who go online, <b id="n-dailyUsers">{N["n-dailyUsers"]}</b> do so every day.</p><p>The gap is in owning a phone. <b id="n-menOwnPhone">{N["n-menOwnPhone"]}</b> of men own one, against <b id="n-womenOwnPhone">{N["n-womenOwnPhone"]}</b> of women. Going online shows a smaller gap: <b id="n-menOnline">{N["n-menOnline"]}</b> of men and <b id="n-womenOnline">{N["n-womenOnline"]}</b> of women. Among people aged 15 to 24, <b id="n-youthOnline">{N["n-youthOnline"]}</b> went online.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Fewer can do more demanding things. <b id="n-bankingAbility">{N["n-bankingAbility"]}</b> of people aged 15 and above say they can pay or bank online, <b id="n-emailAbility">{N["n-emailAbility"]}</b> can send and receive email and <b id="n-wordProcessing">{N["n-wordProcessing"]}</b> can write a document on a computer. Of those who pay online, <b id="n-upiOnly">{N["n-upiOnly"]}</b> use only UPI.</p><p>Shopping online is far less common than going online. <b id="n-onlineShopping">{N["n-onlineShopping"]}</b> of homes bought goods online in the 30 days before the interview: <b id="n-ruralOnlineShopping">{N["n-ruralOnlineShopping"]}</b> in villages and <b id="n-urbanOnlineShopping">{N["n-urbanOnlineShopping"]}</b> in towns.</p></div></div></section>
{groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={[...new Set(model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).flatMap(p=>p.bundles))].join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>{N['pv-'+p.id]}</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone" id={'plot-'+p.id} set:html={digitalChart(p,model)} /><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">National Statistics Office, Comprehensive Modular Survey: Telecom, {p.period}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p>{p.vary==='state'&&<p>States are ranked from highest to lowest; the rule marks the all-India figure. The middle state's figure is <span id={'pm-median-'+p.id}>{digitalFormat(model.plateFacts[p.id].median,p.unit)}</span>; that is the middle of the states, not a national figure.</p>}</div>{p.rows.some(r=>r.quality)&&<details><summary>Homes surveyed and sampling error, by state</summary><p>The number of homes surveyed covers the whole state sample. The sampling error is relative to the estimate itself, not in percentage points, and covers sampling alone.</p><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Homes surveyed</th><th>Sampling error</th></tr></thead><tbody>{p.rows.map(r=><tr><td>{r.name}</td><td>{digitalFormat(r.value,r.unit)}</td><td>{digitalFormat(r.quality?.households,'count')}</td><td>{r.quality?.rse===null?'Not published':digitalFormat(r.quality?.rse,'pct_rse')}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></details>}</div></figure></div>)}</section>)}
<section data-inputs="all digital-access bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"A connected home means everyone is online."</b> The home is counted if anyone can go online there. A woman in a connected home may still have no phone of her own.</p><p><b>"Online means digitally literate."</b> Most people who go online do it on a phone. Far fewer report the skills that forms, banking and complaints need.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"Smartphone share is a share of everyone."</b> It is a share of people who own a phone. People who own no phone are outside it.</p><p><b>"State rankings are exact."</b> State figures rest on small samples. Two states a few points apart may not differ at all.</p></div></div></section>
<EveryCut slug="digital-access"/><GuideData slug="digital-access"/>
<section data-inputs="all digital-access bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>Every figure comes from the National Statistics Office's Comprehensive Modular Survey: Telecom, interviews from January to March 2025. The charts above answer one question each; every published table, state, age group and cut is in the table above and the downloads, together with two earlier surveys on similar questions, the Multiple Indicator Survey of 2020-21 and the Comprehensive Annual Modular Survey of 2022-23. Those used different questions and periods, so they are kept apart and not joined into a trend.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/CMST_report_m.pdf">The survey report</a>. <a href="https://mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/press_release/Final_press%20release_CMS_T.pdf">The press release</a>. Data revision date: <b id="prov-vintage">Unknown</b>; the statistics office does not publish one.</p></section>
</div><script>import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>

<style>details table {width:100%;table-layout:fixed;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.82rem} details th,details td {padding:.3rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #E8EBEF} details p {font-size:.9rem} @media(max-width:700px){details table{font-size:.7rem}details th:first-child{width:38%}}</style>
